## Structs in Go

Structs are composite data types that group together fields of possibly different types.


### Defining a Struct

```go
type Person struct {
    Name string
    Age  int
}
```

### Creating Instances

```go
// Zero value (all fields zero)
p := Person{}

// Literal with fields
p := Person{Name: "Alice", Age: 25}
// Order matters with this syntax
p := Person{"Alice", 25}

// Using new (returns pointer)
p := new(Person) // *Person
```

### Accessing Fields

```go
p.Name = "Bob"
fmt.Println(p.Age)
```

### Pointers to Structs

```go
p := &Person{Name: "Charlie", Age: 35}
p.Age = 36 // Works directly on pointer
```

### Embedded Structs (Like Inheritance)


```go
type Employee struct {
    Person  // embedded
    Company string
}

emp := Employee{
    Person: Person{Name: "Dave"},
    Company: "Acme",
}
fmt.Println(emp.Name) // access embedded field directly
```

### Anonymous Structs

```go
point := struct{X, Y int}{10, 20}
```

